+\paragraph{Boundary-microstate theorem target (v0.0.4).}
+\textbf{[CLOSED-CONDITIONAL / MICROSTATE THEOREM; PHENOMENOLOGICAL COEFFICIENT NOT CLOSED].}
+Let the boundary-relevant SST line fabric be a stationary ergodic line process
+with line-length density $\mathcal L_v$, tangent distribution
+$f(\widehat{\mathbf t})$, and $q\ge2$ protected labels per statistically
+independent piercing. Projection of a line element onto a boundary with unit
+normal $\widehat{\mathbf n}$ gives
+\begin{align}
+    n_\perp(\widehat{\mathbf n})
+    =
+    \mathcal L_v
+    \int_{S^2}
+    |\widehat{\mathbf t}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}|
+    f(\widehat{\mathbf t})\,d\Omega .
+    \label{eq:rt_routeI_nperp_v004}
+\end{align}
+For isotropy, $\langle|\cos\theta|\rangle=1/2$, hence
+\begin{align}
+    n_\perp=\frac{\mathcal L_v}{2}.
+\end{align}
+If each piercing supplies $q$ protected states, then
+\begin{align}
+    W(A)=q^{N_\perp(A)},
+    \qquad
+    S_{\rm cap}(A)=N_\perp(A)\ln q,
+\end{align}
+and ergodicity yields
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{
+    \eta_A^{\rm SST}
+    =
+    n_\perp\ln q
+    }.
+    \label{eq:rt_routeI_eta_v004}
+\end{align}
+
+For an explicit weak-field boundary ensemble, take independent
+unit-covariance Gaussian cells $p_{0,j}=\mathcal N(0,1)$ and
+$p_{\phi,j}=\mathcal N(\mu_j,1)$, with
+\begin{align}
+    \mu_j
+    =
+    \sqrt{4\pi(-U_j)\Delta U\Delta A}
+    \;\partial_U\widehat\phi(U_j).
+\end{align}
+Since
+$D_{\rm KL}(\mathcal N(\mu,1)\Vert\mathcal N(0,1))=\mu^2/2$
+\cite{KullbackLeibler1951}, additivity gives
+\begin{align}
+    \delta S_{\rm boundary}
+    :=
+    D(P_\phi\Vert P_0)
+    =
+    -2\pi
+    \int_{\mathcal H^-}
+    U T_{UU}\,dU\,dA
+    =
+    S_{\rm rel}.
+    \label{eq:rt_routeI_boundary_relative_entropy_v004}
+\end{align}
+This is a relative-entropy identity, not an ordinary entropy difference:
+equal-covariance coherent shifts have equal Shannon/von-Neumann entropy.
+Under an additional reversible capacity-saturating $q$-ary boundary-response
+assumption, $S_{\rm rel}$ nats activate
+$\delta N=S_{\rm rel}/\ln q$ channels. Therefore
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{
+    \eta_A^{\rm SST}\delta A
+    =
+    \delta S_{\rm boundary}
+    =
+    S_{\rm rel}
+    }.
+    \label{eq:rt_routeI_encoding_closure_v004}
+\end{align}
+The operational use of relative entropy as an asymptotic distinguishability
+rate follows the Stein framework \cite{HiaiPetz1991}; the horizon-flux
+identification follows Dorau and Much \cite{DorauMuch2026}.
